  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-asymptotic-distribution": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $d\\ge1$ be fixed. Let $Z_n$ be random elements of the finite-dimensional Euclidean space $\\mathbb R^d$, not necessarily defined on a common probability space, and let $\\nu$ be a Borel probability measure on $\\mathbb R^d$. The sequence $(Z_n)$ has **asymptotic distribution** $\\nu$ if its laws converge weakly to $\\nu$; equivalently, $Z_n\\Rightarrow Z$ for any random vector $Z$ with law $\\nu$ ([[def-convergence-in-distribution-of-random-elements]], [[def-weak-convergence-of-borel-probability-measures]]).\n\nFor an estimator, any centering or scaling, such as $r_n(T_n-\\psi(\\theta))$, is part of the sequence whose asymptotic distribution is being specified; it is not implicit in the term. Weak convergence alone does not assert convergence of moments."
    ],
    "def-op-and-little-op-stochastic-bounds": [
      "## Definition\n\nFor each positive integer $n$, let $(\\Omega_n,\\mathcal A_n,P_n)$ be a probability space and let $X_n:\\Omega_n\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a random vector, where $d\\ge1$ is fixed and $\\mathbb R^d$ has the Euclidean norm $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$ ([[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]], [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]], [[def-probability-measure]]). The spaces and measures may depend on $n$; no common probability space is required.\n\nWrite $X_n=o_P(1)$ if for every $\\varepsilon>0$,\n$$P_n(\\|X_n\\|_2>\\varepsilon)\\longrightarrow0.$$\nWrite $X_n=O_P(1)$, or say that $(X_n)$ is bounded in probability, if for every $\\eta>0$ there is $M>0$ such that\n$$\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}P_n(\\|X_n\\|_2>M)\\le\\eta.$$\nFor real-valued variables on one common probability space, $o_P(1)$ is convergence in probability to zero as defined in [[def-convergence-in-probability]].\n\nThe norms and tail probabilities are part of the definition. Independence is not implicit in either notation."
    ],
    "thm-vector-stochastic-order-calculus": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet all random vectors with index $n$ be defined on a common probability space for that $n$; the spaces may vary with $n$. In any fixed finite-dimensional Euclidean space, the following rules hold:\n\n1. If $U_n=o_P(1)$ and $V_n=o_P(1)$, then $U_n+V_n=o_P(1)$. If $U_n=O_P(1)$ and $V_n=O_P(1)$, then $U_n+V_n=O_P(1)$.\n2. For positive integers $p,q,r$, let $B:\\mathbb R^p\\times\\mathbb R^q\\to\\mathbb R^r$ be a fixed map for which there is a constant $C\\ge0$ with $\\|B(u,v)\\|_2\\le C\\|u\\|_2\\|v\\|_2$ for all $u,v$. If $U_n=o_P(1)$ and $V_n=O_P(1)$, or conversely, then $B(U_n,V_n)=o_P(1)$. If both are $O_P(1)$, then $B(U_n,V_n)=O_P(1)$.\n3. If $X_n\\Rightarrow X$ in $\\mathbb R^d$, then $X_n=O_P(1)$.\n\nThe last conclusion is finite-dimensional boundedness in probability. No independence or Axiom of Choice is assumed."
    ],
    "thm-vector-slutsky": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $d,k,m\\ge1$ be fixed. For each $n$, let $X_n\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $Y_n\\in\\mathbb R^k$ be random vectors on one probability space; the probability space may vary with $n$. Let $X\\in\\mathbb R^d$ be a random vector on any probability space, and let $c\\in\\mathbb R^k$ be constant. If $X_n\\Rightarrow X$ and $Y_n-c=o_P(1)$, then\n$$ (X_n,Y_n)\\Rightarrow(X,c). $$\nConsequently, for every continuous $h:\\mathbb R^{d+k}\\to\\mathbb R^m$, $h(X_n,Y_n)\\Rightarrow h(X,c)$. No independence between $X_n$ and $Y_n$ is required."
    ],
    "def-estimator-sequence-in-iid-experiments": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $\\mathsf P:\\Theta\\to\\mathcal P(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ be a parametric model on a statistical experiment, and let $\\psi:\\Theta\\to\\mathcal T$ be an estimand whose target set $\\mathcal T$ is equipped with a metric $d$ ([[def-statistical-experiment]], [[def-parametric-statistical-model]], [[def-estimand-on-a-statistical-model]], [[def-metric-space]]). For $n\\ge1$, the IID sample-size-$n$ experiment has sample space $\\mathcal X^n$, product sigma-algebra $\\mathcal A^{\\otimes n}$, and sampling law $P_\\theta^{\\otimes n}$ at parameter $\\theta$ ([[def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras]]). The product law is defined recursively from $P_\\theta$; each factor is a probability measure and hence sigma-finite, so the finite product theorem supplies its existence, rectangle formula, and uniqueness ([[thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique]]).\n\nA **nonrandomized estimator sequence** of $\\psi$ is a sequence of measurable maps, in the sense of [[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]],\n$$T_n:(\\mathcal X^n,\\mathcal A^{\\otimes n})\\to(\\mathcal T,\\mathcal B(\\mathcal T)),\\qquad n\\ge1,$$\nwhere $\\mathcal B(\\mathcal T)$ is the Borel sigma-algebra ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]). Its sampling distribution at $\\theta$ and sample size $n$ is the pushforward of $P_\\theta^{\\otimes n}$ by $T_n$.\n\nThis definition fixes IID repeated sampling and data-measurable estimators. It imposes no measurability on the parameter map $\\theta\\mapsto P_\\theta$ or on $\\psi$."
    ],
    "def-consistency-in-probability": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $(T_n)$ be an estimator sequence of an estimand $\\psi$ with metric target $(\\mathcal T,d)$, and fix $\\theta\\in\\Theta$. The sequence is **consistent in probability at $\\theta$** if for every $\\varepsilon>0$,\n$$P_\\theta^{\\otimes n}\\bigl(\\{x\\in\\mathcal X^n:d(T_n(x),\\psi(\\theta))>\\varepsilon\\}\\bigr)\\longrightarrow0.$$\nThe consistency is **pointwise** because $\\theta$ is fixed before the limit. It does not assert uniform convergence over $\\Theta$ ([[def-estimator-sequence-in-iid-experiments]])."
    ],
    "thm-iid-sample-mean-consistency": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\mathcal X=\\mathbb R$ with its Borel sigma-algebra, and let $\\mathsf P$ be a parametric statistical experiment on $\\mathcal X$. Define the mean estimand by $\\mu(\\theta)=\\int x\\,P_\\theta(dx)$ and suppose it is finite for every $\\theta$. For a fixed $\\theta$, suppose an IID sequence $(X_i)_{i\\ge1}$ with one-observation law $P_\\theta$ is given and $\\mathbb E_\\theta|X_1|<\\infty$. The sample-mean estimator $T_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=n^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ is consistent in probability for $\\mu$ at $\\theta$ in the IID sample-size experiments.\n\nThe assertion is pointwise in $\\theta$. The existence of a common probability space carrying the IID sequence is part of the hypothesis."
    ],
    "thm-nonidentifiability-precludes-simultaneous-consistency": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\mathsf P:\\Theta\\to\\mathcal P(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ be a parametric statistical model, let $\\psi:\\Theta\\to(\\mathcal T,d)$ be a metric-valued estimand, and let $(T_n)$ be a nonrandomized estimator sequence for IID samples from this model. If $\\psi$ is not identifiable, then there is a pair of observationally equivalent parameters with distinct target values, and no estimator sequence is consistent in probability at both parameters."
    ],
